938430786,0.0,-0.22027048468589783,0.0,-1.42431640625,-1.0371154530730564e-05,0.0,0.0,0.0,-3.838465272565372e-05,-2.3841855067985307e-07,-2.861018856492592e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,9885,279,2629,315,678,6351,9904,315,279,23606,198,59,26056,37018,90,16,15170,87,61,17,481,220,16,92,488,1124,37018,90,17,15170,87,61,17,481,220,17,92,488,1124,37018,90,18,15170,87,61,17,481,220,18,92,488,1124,37018,90,19,15170,87,61,17,481,220,19,92,284,220,17,15,16,15,87,481,220,19,7110,60,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nFind the sum of all complex solutions of the equation\n\\[\\frac{1}{x^2 - 1} + \\frac{2}{x^2 - 2} + \\frac{3}{x^2 - 3} + \\frac{4}{x^2 - 4} = 2010x - 4.\\]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"b7c41783c81ed7485d275908e3948880c685f7a864d8b1e3851f13d3f7c8669b"},"seconds":27.881038665771484,"seed":1093890960725440505,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the equation\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{x^2 - 1} + \\frac{2}{x^2 - 2} + \\frac{3}{x^2 - 3} + \\frac{4}{x^2 - 4} = 2010x - 4,\n\\]\nwe start by considering the left-hand side of the equation. Each term on the left-hand side is a rational function with a pole at \\(x = \\pm \\sqrt{n}\\) for \\(n = 1, 2, 3, 4\\). The right-hand side is a linear function of \\(x\\).\n\nLet \\(y = x^2\\). Then the equation becomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{y - 1} + \\frac{2}{y - 2} + \\frac{3}{y - 3} + \\frac{4}{y - 4} = 2010x - 4.\n\\]\nThis equation is a function of \\(y\\) on the left and a function of \\(x\\) on the right. Since the left side is a rational function of \\(y\\) and the right side is a linear function of \\(x\\), we need to consider the symmetry and the degrees of the polynomials involved.\n\nThe sum of the roots of the equation in terms of \\(x\\) can be found by considering the symmetry of the equation. Notice that if \\(x\\) is a solution, then \\(-x\\) is also a solution because the left-hand side of the equation is an even function of \\(x\\) (since it depends only on \\(x^2\\)). Thus, the solutions come in pairs \\((x, -x)\\).\n\nIf we let the solutions be \\(x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n\\), then the sum of all solutions is:\n\\[\nx_1 + x_2 + \\cdots + x_n = 0,\n\\]\nbecause for each positive solution \\(x_i\\), there is a corresponding negative solution \\(-x_i\\).\n\nTherefore, the sum of all complex solutions of the equation is \\(\\boxed{0}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{0}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
